<MorpheusModel version="4">
  <Description>
    <Title>Atwell 2015: C. elegans gonad development and mechanical feedback</Title>
    <Details>Source: Atwell et al., Development 142:3902-3911 (2015), doi:10.1242/dev.126359.
Source model: 3D off-lattice deformable spheres in Chaste plus orthogonal cell statecharts.
This Morpheus translation uses actual 3D CPM cells, initially 16; daughters are generated only by division.
Hours post-hatching are the simulation time units; a lattice node is 1 micrometre.
Preserved: stage-dependent DTC migration and radial growth, 5 um DTC contact requirement,
transverse semicircular turn (radius 11.5 um), late-L4 proximal stretching, distal/turn rachis,
irreversible GLP-1 loss at 70 um, G1 commitment, one final mitosis for S/G2 cells,
G1/S/G2/M timing (2/57/39/2%), larval/adult 3/8 h cycle, stochastic G1/G2,
adult G2 compression arrest below 70% rest volume, 200 um sex decision, 32.5 h fate switch,
two sperm divisions after 2 h, 1 um/h meiotic/oogenic radius growth, stage-dependent apoptosis,
and paired sperm/oocyte removal at the proximal outlet.
Approximations: CPM volume/surface regularization supports the contact-force adapter described below;
finite boundary/lumen energy replaces center-position projection. The DTC is an explicit global
leader coordinate and trajectory state, rather than a CPM cell. No axial cell motility bias is used.
CPM energy parameters are numerical choices; the source spring constant mu=50 enters the force adapter.
Counts and cell-resolved diagnostics are recorded independently of target images or count data.
See evidence/model_grounding.md and scripts/build_model.py for definitions and provenance.
Source-force extension: overlapping CPM neighbours contribute the published logarithmic
repulsion with mu=50; force components are passed to CPM cell displacement with radius-dependent
mobility 5/(eta*r), eta=1. This is an isotropic, neighbour-derived force, not an active axial cue.
The CPM contact neighbourhood approximates the source all-overlapping-spheres neighbour set,
and energy coupling substitutes for direct off-lattice Euler integration. The coupling scale is
a declared numerical adapter parameter. The source effective-radius compression estimator
is used for the adult 70 percent checkpoint: Reff = r - sum(overlap)/(2*max(12,Noverlap)).
The compressed/rest volume ratio is (Reff/r)^3; occupied CPM volume is logged separately.
All earlier CPM/boundary approximations remain.
Division planes have random 3D orientation, matching the default division directions in the paper-era Chaste OffLatticeSimulation (release_3.3). CPM splits a mother lattice cell rather than placing two overlapping sphere centres 0.3 um apart. Prescribed geometry fields stop recomputing after the gonad becomes stationary in adulthood; saved fields retain their final values.
Centre-constraint extension: each cell computes the nearest point on the reported midline.
In the distal arm and turn, its restoring displacement projects the centre onto radius R-r;
in the proximal arm, it only acts if the centre is outside radius R-r. The existing numerical
wall-energy weight converts this restoring displacement to a CPM bias together with the
source repulsion. This is a soft approximation to the source hard projection, has no active
axial motility term, and does not assign fates or cell counts. It addresses cross-arm mixing
permitted by voxel confinement alone. Existing boundary fields still restrict voxel extent.</Details>
  </Description>
